# DAM Soft Delete (Trash)

The Soft Delete feature provides a safety net for accidental deletions of assets and collections in Digital Asset Management (DAM). Instead of permanently removing items immediately, a soft delete moves them to a **Trash** state where they are hidden from normal views but remain in the database for a configurable period. Items in the Trash can be restored or permanently deleted by authorized users.

## Soft Delete Lifecycle

### Moving an item to Trash

When a user soft deletes an asset or collection, the item transitions from **Active** to **Soft Deleted** (Trash). The item is not removed from the database; instead, an `is_deleted` flag is set on the record along with the deletion timestamp and the identity of the user who initiated the action.

To move an item to Trash:

1. Open the overflow menu for the asset or collection.
2. Select **Move to Trash**.
3. Confirm the action in the confirmation dialog.
4. A snackbar notification confirms the move and provides a link to view the Trash.

Soft-deleted items are hidden from all standard list, search, and fetch APIs. They remain accessible only through the Trash view.

Items in the Trash are automatically and permanently deleted after a configurable retention period (default: 30 days). The **Days left to be permanently deleted** value is displayed on each asset in the Trash view. For collections, this information is available via the info (**i**) icon.

### Trash visibility and permissions

Access to the Trash is restricted by role:

| Role | Trash Access |
|---|---|
| Collection Admin | Can view, restore, and permanently delete items in the Trash. Can also clear the entire Trash. |
| Editor / Standard User | Cannot view or access the Trash. |

The Trash can be accessed from **Settings** or via the snackbar notification that appears after a soft delete.

## Restore Logic and Conflict Handling

### Standard restore

A collection admin can restore individual assets or entire collections from the Trash. Restoring a collection restores the collection itself, but does **not** automatically restore sub-collections that were independently moved to the Trash before the parent was deleted.

### Dependency rules

- If a sub-collection or asset is in the Trash and its parent collection is also in the Trash, the sub-item **cannot** be restored until the parent collection is restored first.
- If the parent collection no longer exists (was permanently deleted), the child items cannot be restored.

### Name conflict resolution

If a new item with the same name has been created in the same location after the original was soft deleted, a name conflict occurs during restoration. In this case:

1. The system detects the collision and prompts the user with a **Rename** option.
2. The user can provide a new name to complete the restore.
3. If the user does not rename the item, the restore operation is cancelled.

## Permanent Deletion

### Individual permanent delete

A collection admin can permanently delete a specific item from the Trash at any time, bypassing the configured retention period. This action is irreversible.

### Clear Trash (Empty Trash)

A collection admin can clear all items from the Trash that they have admin access to. This permanently removes all those items immediately.

### Automated Trash clearance (heartbeat)

A background heartbeat runs at a configured interval to automatically purge items whose retention period has expired. This ensures that the Trash is cleared regularly without manual intervention.

## Download, Preview, and Properties in Trash

Assets in the Trash retain their binary data during the retention period. The following operations are available for items in the Trash:

- **Preview**: View the asset content.
- **Download**: Download the original asset file.
- **Properties**: View the asset or collection metadata.

These operations are read-only; items in the Trash cannot be edited.

## Configuration

The Soft Delete and Trash clearance behavior is controlled via `values.yaml` under the `configuration.digitalAssetManagement` section:

```yaml
configuration:
  digitalAssetManagement:
    # Enable or disable automatic Trash clearance
    enableTrashClearance: true
    # Number of days before items in the Trash are automatically and permanently deleted
    trashClearanceTimeInDays: 30
    # Interval (in minutes) at which the Trash clearance heartbeat runs
    trashClearanceHeartbeatInMinutes: 60
```

!!!note
    When `enableSoftDelete` is set to `false`, delete operations immediately and permanently remove the records (hard delete). No items are moved to the Trash.

## Cross-Environment Synchronization (Publisher/Subscriber)

When DAM is used in a Publisher/Subscriber staging topology, soft delete actions on the Publisher propagate to Subscribers. The behavior depends on the Cumulative Fix (CF) level of the Subscriber environment:

| Action on Publisher | Impact on Subscriber (Modern CF) | Impact on Subscriber (Legacy CF) |
|---|---|---|
| **Soft Delete** | Item is Soft Deleted (moved to Trash) | Item is Hard Deleted immediately |
| **Restore** | Item is Restored to Active state | Item is Re-created |
| **Permanent Delete** | Item is Hard Deleted | N/A (already deleted on legacy) |

!!!note
    Legacy CF Subscribers do not support the Soft Delete state. A Soft Delete action on the Publisher results in an immediate hard delete on those Subscribers. Restoration on the Publisher re-creates the item on the Legacy CF Subscriber.

## Limitations

- An asset or collection cannot be restored from the Trash if its parent collection is also in the Trash. Restore the parent collection first.
- An asset or collection cannot be restored if an item with the same name already exists in the same location. Use the Rename option to resolve the conflict.
- Search and filter are not available within the Trash view.
- Multiple-select for bulk delete or bulk restore in the Trash is not supported.
- Collections in the Trash are not traversable; their contents cannot be browsed while the collection is soft deleted.

